{"name":"Gruyère Cheese","hex":"#f5deb3","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"cheese","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A dairy product made from curdled or cultured milk.","Any particular variety of cheese."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To prepare curds for making cheese.","To make holes in a pattern of circuitry to decrease pattern density."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/cheese"}],"entries":[{"title":"Gruyère cheese","blocks":[{"text":"Gruyère is a hard Swiss cheese that originated in the Fribourg, Vaud, Neuchâtel, Jura, and Bern cantons in Switzerland. It is named after the town of Gruyères in Fribourg. In 2001, Gruyère gained the appellation d'origine contrôlée (AOC), which became the appellation d'origine protégée (AOP) as of 2013."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/Gruyere_alpage_th_wa.jpg/330px-Gruyere_alpage_th_wa.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Gruy%C3%A8re_cheese","wikidata":"Q2376488"},{"title":"Cheese","blocks":[{"text":"Cheese is a type of dairy product produced in a range of flavors, textures, and forms by curdling of milk, usually from cows, goats or sheep, and sometimes of water buffalo. During production, milk is usually acidified and either the enzymes of rennet or bacterial enzymes with similar activity are added to cause the protein casein to coagulate. The solid curds, consisting of proteins and milkfat, are then separated from the liquid whey and pressed into finished cheese. Some cheeses have aromatic molds on the outer layer or rind, while others have them throughout."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/a8/Cheese_platter.jpg/330px-Cheese_platter.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Cheese","wikidata":"Q10943"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons